# Redirects for the static build.
#
# `redirects()` in next.config.ts only runs on a server. These mirror it, in
# the format Cloudflare Pages and Netlify both read from the output root.
# Keep in sync with `redirectRules()` in next.config.ts.
#
# The apex -> www redirect is NOT here. On Cloudflare it belongs in the
# dashboard as a Redirect Rule, because _redirects is matched per-path after
# the request has already reached this origin and cannot match on hostname.
# Setting up both hostnames to serve the same Pages project without a rule
# would put the whole site on two origins again, which is the duplicate-content
# problem the server-side rule existed to close. See the deployment notes in
# docs/ for the exact dashboard steps.

# /tech duplicated /technologies and re-listed vendor names that were
# deliberately removed as unverifiable. It was in the sitemap and the footer.
/tech  /technologies  301

# Renamed product: "Server Fleet Dashboard" -> "Unified iDRAC Management".
# The old path was in the sitemap and linked from /work.
/products/infrastructure-management  /products/unified-idrac-management  301

# No rules for /api/* or /admin. An earlier version sent them to /404 with a
# 404 status, which Cloudflare rejects outright — _redirects accepts only 200,
# 301, 302, 303, 307 and 308, and the whole file is discarded if any line uses
# anything else. That is what failed the first deploy.
#
# They are not needed anyway: those paths do not exist in the output, and
# Cloudflare serves the exported 404.html for anything unmatched, which is the
# honest status code and the right page.

# Google Search Console ownership file. Google fetches it at exactly this
# address and wants the file itself, but Cloudflare's auto-trailing-slash
# handling answers any *.html request with a 307 to the extensionless URL.
# A 200 rule serves the file in place. Do not delete the file or this rule:
# Google re-checks, and removing either unverifies the property.
/googlee5eb4e82f10924bc.html  /googlee5eb4e82f10924bc  200
